{
    "background_story": "Carolina was a skilled seamstress who worked at the Triangle Shirtwaist Factory in New York City in the early 1900s. She took great pride in her work, carefully crafting each shirtwaist dress with precision and attention to detail. Despite the long hours and poor working conditions, Carolina found joy in seeing the finished garments, knowing they would bring happiness to the women who wore them. However, her life took a dramatic turn on March 25, 1911, when a devastating fire swept through the factory, trapping many workers inside. Carolina's bravery and quick thinking helped save several of her coworkers, and she became a symbol of resilience and strength in the face of tragedy. Her story was instrumental in highlighting the need for improved labor laws and worker safety regulations.",
